    <div class="container">
        <div class="header">
            <h1>Active Satellites in Earth Orbit — 2026 Catalog Analysis</h1>
            <p class="subtitle">A comprehensive analytical report on the 14,875 active satellites currently orbiting Earth</p>
            <p class="date">Report Generated: May 2026 | Data Source: CelesTrak / NORAD TLE Catalog</p>
        </div>

        <div class="kpi-grid">
            <div class="kpi-card highlight">
                <div class="value">14,875</div>
                <div class="label">Total Active Satellites</div>
            </div>
            <div class="kpi-card">
                <div class="value">86.5%</div>
                <div class="label">LEO Share (MEAN_MOTION &gt; 14)</div>
            </div>
            <div class="kpi-card">
                <div class="value">10,083</div>
                <div class="label">Starlink Constellation</div>
            </div>
            <div class="kpi-card">
                <div class="value">53.2°</div>
                <div class="label">Median Inclination</div>
            </div>
            <div class="kpi-card">
                <div class="value">13,461</div>
                <div class="label">Launched Since 2020 (90.5%)</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="section">
            <h2>1. Catalog Overview & Key Figures</h2>
            <p>This dataset represents a real-time catalog of <strong>14,875 active satellites</strong> currently orbiting Earth, sourced from the CelesTrak space-tracking platform via NORAD Two-Line Element (TLE) data. The catalog covers operational spacecraft ranging from mega-constellations like Starlink to individual science missions, weather satellites, GPS navigation satellites, and the International Space Station.</p>

            <div class="stat-row">
                <div class="stat-item">
                    <div class="stat-value">1964 – 2026</div>
                    <div class="stat-label">Epoch Range (first launch to present)</div>
                </div>
                <div class="stat-item">
                    <div class="stat-value">12860</div>
                    <div class="stat-label">Satellites in LEO</div>
                </div>
                <div class="stat-item">
                    <div class="stat-value">596</div>
                    <div class="stat-label">Satellites in GEO</div>
                </div>
                <div class="stat-item">
                    <div class="stat-value">1388</div>
                    <div class="stat-label">Satellites in MEO/HEO</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="insight-box">
                <strong>Key Finding:</strong> Starlink dominates the active satellite population with 10,083 satellites (67.8% of all active satellites). This single constellation exceeds the entire non-Starlink population of 4,792 satellites.
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="section">
            <h2>2. Orbital Regime Classification</h2>
            <p>Satellites are classified into orbital regimes using <strong>MEAN_MOTION thresholds</strong>: LEO (&gt;14 rev/day), GEO (0.9–1.1 rev/day), and MEO/HEO (all other orbits including highly eccentric paths).</p>

            <div class="chart-container">
                <canvas id="regimeChart"></canvas>
            </div>

            <table>
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th>Orbital Regime</th>
                        <th>MEAN_MOTION Threshold</th>
                        <th>Count</th>
                        <th>Percentage</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>LEO</strong> (Low Earth Orbit)</td>
                        <td>&gt; 14 rev/day</td>
                        <td>12,860</td>
                        <td>86.45%</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>MEO/HEO</strong> (Medium/Highly Elliptical)</td>
                        <td>1.5–14 rev/day (or e &gt; 0.3)</td>
                        <td>1,419</td>
                        <td>9.54%</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>GEO</strong> (Geostationary)</td>
                        <td>0.9–1.1 rev/day</td>
                        <td>596</td>
                        <td>4.01%</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>

            <p class="note">Note: LEO spans approximately 160–2,000 km altitude. GEO orbits at ~35,786 km, matching Earth's rotation. HEO includes Molniya-type and other highly elliptical orbits (36 satellites with eccentricity &gt; 0.3).</p>
        </div>

        <div class="section">
            <h2>3. Major Constellation & Operator Breakdown</h2>
            <p>The constellation landscape is dominated by SpaceX's Starlink, which reshapes nearly every orbital statistic. Below is the breakdown of satellite operators and constellations.</p>

            <div class="chart-container">
                <canvas id="constellationChart"></canvas>
            </div>

            <table>
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th>Constellation / Operator</th>
                        <th>Satellite Count</th>
                        <th>Share of Total</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr style="background: #fff3f3;">
                        <td><strong>Starlink</strong></td>
                        <td>10,083</td>
                        <td>67.78%</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Other (individual satellites)</td>
                        <td>3,802</td>
                        <td>25.56%</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>OneWeb</td>
                        <td>651</td>
                        <td>4.38%</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Cosmos (Russian government)</td>
                        <td>126</td>
                        <td>0.85%</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Iridium</td>
                        <td>80</td>
                        <td>0.54%</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Beidou</td>
                        <td>55</td>
                        <td>0.37%</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Intelsat</td>
                        <td>28</td>
                        <td>0.19%</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>SES</td>
                        <td>20</td>
                        <td>0.13%</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Orbcomm</td>
                        <td>15</td>
                        <td>0.10%</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>

            <div class="insight-box">
                <strong>Starlink's Dominance:</strong> With 10,083 of 14,875 active satellites, Starlink accounts for nearly 68% of the entire active population. All Starlink satellites are in LEO (MEAN_MOTION &gt; 14). The constellation's rapid expansion since 2019 has fundamentally altered the orbital environment.
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="section">
            <h2>4. Launch Year Trends</h2>
            <p>Analysis of the active satellite population by launch year reveals a dramatic acceleration in deployment activity. <strong>90.5% of all active satellites</strong> were launched since 2020, with 2025 alone accounting for 4,329 satellites.</p>

            <div class="chart-container">
                <canvas id="launchTrendChart"></canvas>
            </div>

            <div class="chart-container">
                <canvas id="cumulativeChart"></canvas>
            </div>

            <h3>Launch Activity by Year</h3>
            <table>
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th>Year</th>
                        <th>New Satellites</th>
                        <th>Cumulative Total</th>
                        <th>Year-over-Year Growth</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>2017</td><td>136</td><td>1,063</td><td>—</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>2018</td><td>200</td><td>1,263</td><td>+47%</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>2019</td><td>151</td><td>1,414</td><td>-24%</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>2020</td><td>498</td><td>1,912</td><td>+230%</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>2021</td><td>1,063</td><td>2,975</td><td>+114%</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>2022</td><td>1,804</td><td>4,779</td><td>+70%</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>2023</td><td>2,328</td><td>7,107</td><td>+29%</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>2024</td><td>2,477</td><td>9,584</td><td>+6%</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>2025</td><td>4,329</td><td>13,913</td><td>+75%</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>2026 (YTD)</td><td>962</td><td>14,875</td><td>—</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>

            <div class="stat-row">
                <div class="stat-item">
                    <div class="stat-value">13,461</div>
                    <div class="stat-label">Satellites launched 2020–2026</div>
                </div>
                <div class="stat-item">
                    <div class="stat-value">90.5%</div>
                    <div class="stat-label">Share of active fleet</div>
                </div>
                <div class="stat-item">
                    <div class="stat-value">75%</div>
                    <div class="stat-label">YoY growth in 2025</div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="section">
            <h2>5. Orbital Parameter Deep Dive</h2>

            <div class="two-col">
                <div>
                    <h3>Inclination Distribution</h3>
                    <p>Orbital inclination measures the satellite's orbital tilt relative to Earth's equator. The median inclination is <strong>53.2°</strong>, indicating that many constellations (including Starlink's shell designs) operate at mid-latitude orbits.</p>

                    <div class="chart-container">
                        <canvas id="inclinationChart"></canvas>
                    </div>

                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Inclination Range</th>
                                <th>Satellite Count</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>0° – 30° (Equatorial)</td><td>650</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>30° – 45° (Low-mid)</td><td>3,880</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>45° – 60° (Mid)</td><td>5,570</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>60° – 75° (Mid-high)</td><td>867</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>75° – 90° (Polar approach)</td><td>1,078</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>90° – 105° (Polar crossing)</td><td>2,816</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div>
                    <h3>Mean Motion Distribution</h3>
                    <p>Mean motion (revolutions per day) characterizes the orbital altitude. LEO satellites (14–16 rev/day) dominate at <strong>11,781 satellites (79.2%)</strong>.</p>

                    <div class="chart-container">
                        <canvas id="meanMotionChart"></canvas>
                    </div>

                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Mean Motion (rev/day)</th>
                                <th>Count</th>
                                <th>Orbital Region</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>&lt; 1</td><td>32</td><td>Beyond GEO / Deep space</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>0.9 – 1.1</td><td>596</td><td>GEO</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>1.5 – 14</td><td>1,466</td><td>MEO</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>14 – 15</td><td>1,767</td><td>LEO (lower)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>15 – 16</td><td>11,014</td><td>LEO (primary)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </div>

            <h3>Eccentricity Analysis</h3>
            <p>Most satellites have near-circular orbits (eccentricity &lt; 0.1). However, <strong>36 satellites</strong> have highly elliptical orbits (e &gt; 0.3), characteristic of HEO regimes such as Molniya orbits used for high-latitude communications.</p>

            <div class="stat-row">
                <div class="stat-item">
                    <div class="stat-value">14,834</div>
                    <div class="stat-label">Circular/near-circular (e &lt; 0.1)</div>
                </div>
                <div class="stat-item">
                    <div class="stat-value">36</div>
                    <div class="stat-label">Highly elliptical (e &gt; 0.3)</div>
                </div>
                <div class="stat-item">
                    <div class="stat-value">0.000156</div>
                    <div class="stat-label">Median eccentricity</div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="section">
            <h2>6. Key Takeaways & Outlook</h2>

            <div class="insight-box">
                <h3 style="margin-top: 0;">1. Starlink Has Reshaped Earth's Orbital Environment</h3>
                <p>SpaceX's Starlink constellation now comprises 67.8% of all active satellites. This single operator has transformed LEO into a heavily congested domain, with implications for spectrum allocation, collision risk, and regulatory frameworks.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="insight-box">
                <h3 style="margin-top: 0;">2. Launch Acceleration is Unprecedented</h3>
                <p>The active satellite population has grown from ~1,400 in 2019 to nearly 15,000 in 2026 — a 10x increase in just seven years. The pace continues to accelerate, with 2025 setting a record at 4,329 new satellites.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="insight-box">
                <h3 style="margin-top: 0;">3. LEO Dominates All Orbital Regimes</h3>
                <p>86.5% of active satellites are in LEO, driven by mega-constellation deployment. GEO (4.0%) remains critical for communications and weather but is a small fraction of total activity. MEO/HEO (9.5%) hosts navigation constellations (GPS, GLONASS, Galileo, Beidou).</p>
            </div>

            <div class="insight-box">
                <h3 style="margin-top: 0;">4. Orbital Shell Concentrations Create Collision Concerns</h3>
                <p>The 53.2° median inclination and dense LEO shells (11,014 satellites at 15–16 rev/day) indicate concentrated orbital volumes. Collision avoidance and debris mitigation have become operational necessities rather than theoretical concerns.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="insight-box">
